                  </mods:role>
                  <mods:namePart>Grupo de Investigación en Psiquiatría GIPSI</mods:namePart>
               </mods:name>
               <mods:name>
                  <mods:role>
                     <mods:roleTerm type="text">researchgroup</mods:roleTerm>
                  </mods:role>
                  <mods:namePart>Grupo de Investigación en Psiquiatría GIPSI</mods:namePart>
               </mods:name>
               <mods:extension>
                  <mods:dateAccessioned encoding="iso8601">2022-11-21T16:23:52Z</mods:dateAccessioned>
               </mods:extension>
               <mods:extension>
                  <mods:dateAvailable encoding="iso8601">2022-11-21T16:23:52Z</mods:dateAvailable>
               </mods:extension>
               <mods:originInfo>
                  <mods:dateIssued encoding="iso8601">2014</mods:dateIssued>
               </mods:originInfo>
               <mods:identifier type="issn">1177-5467</mods:identifier>
               <mods:identifier type="uri">https://hdl.handle.net/10495/32173</mods:identifier>
               <mods:identifier type="doi">10.2147/OPTH.S72549</mods:identifier>
               <mods:identifier type="eissn">1177-5483</mods:identifier>
               <mods:abstract>ABSTRACT: Refractive errors are a fairly common eye condition worldwide, and the ophthalmologist should be capable of offering the patient both nonsurgical and surgical solutions to their refractive conditions. Nevertheless, currently, refractive surgery training during residency is poor at best. This paper explores recent evidence to suggest that postsurgical results of patients operated on by residents are not inferior to those operated on by experienced staff. It points out the urgent need to improve the current approach to refractive surgery training.</mods:abstract>
               <mods:language>
                  <mods:languageTerm authority="rfc3066">eng</mods:languageTerm>
               </mods:language>
               <mods:accessCondition type="useAndReproduction"/>
               <mods:titleInfo>
                  <mods:title>Refractive surgery training during residency : do not be afraid of the dark</mods:title>
               </mods:titleInfo>
               <mods:genre>Artículo de revista</mods:genre>
            </mods:mods>
         </xmlData>
      </mdWrap>
   </dmdSec>
